WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.800
What's creepier than a clown? An&nbsp;
alien spider from another dimension.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.570
Pennywise's origins are no laughing matter.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.585
The inspiration for Pennywise came to Stephen&nbsp;
King while he was crossing a covered bridge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.440
In Bev Vincent's "Stephen King: A&nbsp;
Complete Exploration of His Work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.960
Life, and Influences," the iconic&nbsp;
horror writer is quoted as saying,

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.920
"I was wearing cowboy boots with rundown heels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:29.920
and I was very aware of the sound they made on&nbsp;
the boards; they sounded like a hollow clock."

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.000
His mind recalled the fairy tale "Three&nbsp;
Billy Goats Gruff," and the troll dwelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.200
below a bridge threatening to&nbsp;
gobble up three goat brothers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.080
He subsequently imagined an evil force that&nbsp;
could shapeshift into anything its prey feared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.680
giving King the freedom to write about&nbsp;
all the monsters he could think of.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.760
According to Vincent's book, the idea to&nbsp;
make Pennywise a clown struck King while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.880
he walked the streets of Bangor, Maine after&nbsp;
a rainstorm. While passing a sewer, his mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.560
once again, went to that troll under&nbsp;
the bridge. King is quoted as saying,

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.000
"As I looked at this one sewer as I was going&nbsp;
by, the voice inside speaks up again and says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.693
'The troll lives there too, only when&nbsp;
he's in the sewers he has a clown suit."'

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.840
“You hear voices from the sewer?”

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.800
It's common knowledge that most of Stephen&nbsp;
King's books and short stories are connected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.520
with the author creating an intricate shared&nbsp;
universe for much of his career. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.200
a television series set in the fictional&nbsp;
town of Castle Rock brought the author's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.520
various characters and stories together&nbsp;
to tell brand new tales for two seasons.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.760
If you're yet to read any King books, you&nbsp;
may not be aware that his stories actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.280
take place in a multiverse. Importantly,&nbsp;
there is another dark space outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.920
that multiverse called the Macroverse. In his&nbsp;
"Dark Tower" cycle of books, this is referred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.040
to as the Todash Darkness and some pretty freaky&nbsp;
things beyond our comprehension live out there.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.560
One of its inhabitants is a gargantuan cosmic&nbsp;
turtle called Maturin who vomited up our universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.720
like something out of an ancient creation&nbsp;
myth. Maturin isn't all that interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.360
what happens to us, which is why another entity&nbsp;
from the Macroverse was able to enter our space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.920
crash into an area that would become Derry,&nbsp;
Maine, and feed on humanity for centuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.540
The second being is what readers of "It"&nbsp;
know as Pennywise, the Eater of Worlds.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.920
Fiction contains a lot of monsters driven&nbsp;
by hunger. Zombies often shamble through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.160
post-apocalyptic worlds looking for living&nbsp;
flesh to consume while vampires stalk the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.560
night thirsting for blood. While all these&nbsp;
monsters have their own creative strengths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.680
and weaknesses, Pennywise stands apart because of&nbsp;
the sheer joy it derives from hunting its prey.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.200
The entity we refer to as Pennywise doesn't just&nbsp;
kill and eat its food, but toys with it first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.960
For It, eating isn't enough, and the point is to&nbsp;
savor the meal. Like a chef perfectly preparing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:48.080
a steak by tenderizing and salting it just&nbsp;
enough, Pennywise preps its food by scaring it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.240
As a result, the scenes in which Pennywise&nbsp;
takes the form of a werewolf, the Creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:57.516
from the Black Lagoon, or a sick and rotting&nbsp;
old man, are It's version of "salting the meat."

00:02:57.622 --> 00:03:01.293
"I can smell the stink of your fear."

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.280
The flesh of a scared person tastes better&nbsp;
than that of someone who doesn't suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.920
they're about to be eaten. This is why It&nbsp;
takes on so many forms, in order to exploit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.720
a person's fear and bring out their very best&nbsp;
flavors. Children naturally scare more easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.520
than adults because they still believe in the&nbsp;
impossible things that adults lose faith in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.360
For an adult, believing in supernatural&nbsp;
monsters is a psychological defect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.920
For kids, belief in monsters is a means of&nbsp;
survival. The monster under the bed can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.261
get you if you know it's there. Unless, of&nbsp;
course, it decides to come looking for you.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.234
“I thought if maybe you couldn’t see my&nbsp;
face, maybe you’d want to be my friend.”

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.440
After crashing into our planet from the&nbsp;
Macroverse, It slept deep below the ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.600
until the early part of the 16th century,&nbsp;
when mankind started inhabiting the land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.640
above his domain. Awakened from a long, deep&nbsp;
slumber, it was hungry and began devouring the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.280
people going about their lives in this new&nbsp;
world born from Maturin. After about a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:05.360
of doing so, It was satiated and returned to its&nbsp;
subterranean home to sleep for another 27 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:09.920
This was the beginning of a cycle of horror&nbsp;
that would continue for more than two centuries.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.080
Pennywise's natural feeding routine is&nbsp;
to wake up every 27 years and eat the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.560
residents of Derry before hibernating. During&nbsp;
this time, the small town goes pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:23.680
unnoticed by the rest of the world, allowing It&nbsp;
to feed without gaining unnecessary attention.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:28.000
Inhabitants of Derry seem to forget the&nbsp;
entity's terrible deeds shortly after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.880
they occur. This is why the Losers' Club&nbsp;
have no idea Pennywise even exists when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.760
he starts coming after them in 1957.&nbsp;
One resident remembers seeing Pennywise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.360
the night a lumberjack killed a dozen&nbsp;
men at the dawn of the 20th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:47.200
but didn't know It's name and never told anyone&nbsp;
else. It is this kind of traumatic amnesia that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.337
allows Pennywise to continue feeding unchecked,&nbsp;
until some kids decide to finally fight back.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.800
Obviously, there is nothing realistic about&nbsp;
Pennywise, but that doesn't mean that everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.960
about King's novel is complete fiction. One&nbsp;
of the most horrific scenes in the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.520
and brought to life in "It: Chapter Two,"&nbsp;
involves a homophobic hate crime where a gay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.720
man is beaten and thrown off a bridge. He has&nbsp;
asthma and struggles to keep from drowning. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.600
make things worse, the individual offering him&nbsp;
a helping hand is Pennywise the dancing clown.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.640
The scene was inspired by the actual 1984&nbsp;
murder of Charlie Howard. In "Stephen King:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.120
A Complete Exploration of His Work, Life,&nbsp;
and Influences," Bev Vincent writes,

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:29.520
"The victim (Howard) did not drown after being&nbsp;
thrown off the bridge into the stream — he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.160
suffocated because of his asthma, which&nbsp;
King likens to being frightened to death."

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.160
According to Vincent, King researched the&nbsp;
police investigation of Charlie Howard's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.280
murder and created a fictionalized version of&nbsp;
it for the portion of his book set in 1985.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.800
The decision to include the scene in&nbsp;
"It: Chapter Two" was controversial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.640
with some regarding it as cheap and&nbsp;
disrespectful. However, director Andy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.880
Muschietti and star Jessica Chastain felt&nbsp;
it was important to include the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.352
because Pennywise represents the darkness&nbsp;
in humans that society prefers to ignore.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.520
As Pennywise is an ancient entity from&nbsp;
another universe, It isn't limited to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:08.560
the same rules as the rest of us. Instead,&nbsp;
the true form of It is unknowable. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.640
we do know that the deadlights seem to&nbsp;
be a kind of power source driving It,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:16.240
and anyone who sees that power&nbsp;
is driven insane. In the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.200
Bill's wife sees them and becomes catatonic&nbsp;
until Pennywise is defeated. This energy also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:26.640
provides It with a vast array of abilities only&nbsp;
glimpsed in the film and television adaptations.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.200
Shapeshifting and mindreading are the obvious&nbsp;
examples. We know that Pennywise can dip into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.000
a person's consciousness, identify what scares&nbsp;
them most, and then become that thing. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.400
it's a lot less clear how It has the ability&nbsp;
to control people. For instance, why don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.600
Derry residents remember when Pennywise kills and&nbsp;
eats their friends and family? And why is it that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.634
when people move away from Derry, their childhoods&nbsp;
become something akin to barely remembered dreams?

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.053
"I forgot. How could I forget?"

00:06:57.053 --> 00:07:01.120
Pennywise's ability to control people like&nbsp;
puppets, while also manipulating their thoughts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.400
is truly terrifying. That manipulation runs&nbsp;
even deeper, with It seemingly able to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.680
the town of Derry alive and thriving&nbsp;
despite all the murders that happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:15.280
every 27 years. By manipulating the town's&nbsp;
reality, It is able to maintain an endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.352
food supply. The spell over Derry only begins&nbsp;
to break when Pennywise is finally defeated.

00:07:20.688 --> 00:07:25.360
Readers of King's novel join the Losers' Club&nbsp;
on a journey from childhood to adulthood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.600
experiencing the characters' fears and&nbsp;
heartbreaks for hundreds of pages. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:33.920
everything comes down to the group standing up&nbsp;
against Pennywise's most terrifying form yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.640
which is a giant spider. While some might find&nbsp;
the villain's final form a little disappointing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.920
this isn't just any spider,&nbsp;
but a giant pregnant spider.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:45.440
Suddenly, everything we thought we knew&nbsp;
about Pennywise is thrown out the window,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:47.840
because it appears as though&nbsp;
the being can reproduce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.400
Although it's assumed that the spider&nbsp;
is Pennywise's true form, that doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.360
necessarily confirm that It is actually pregnant.&nbsp;
This could be another tactic to frighten the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.680
Losers' Club into thinking there are more of&nbsp;
its kind out there, whether there are or not.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.760
The fact that the book makes us ask these&nbsp;
questions proves that King really tapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:10.000
into something special with Pennywise's final&nbsp;
form. When the book is finished, we have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.600
idea what was real and what wasn't. Was the being&nbsp;
actually female, and did any of those eggs hatch?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.080
More importantly, were those eggs even&nbsp;
real? The doubt that we're instilled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:25.280
is actually the point. As the product of a&nbsp;
person's fears and anxieties, it's poetic that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:30.900
readers and viewers are left with the unsettling&nbsp;
notion that It might not actually be gone.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:34.480
For any reader really interested in&nbsp;
wandering through Stephen King's multiverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:38.880
there's no better place to do so than&nbsp;
in "The Dark Tower" books. The series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:43.840
is about a gunslinger named Roland Deschain on&nbsp;
a quest to find the Dark Tower at the center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:48.560
of the multiverse and climb to the top of it.&nbsp;
While doing so, he travels between realities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:53.040
encountering characters from other King books&nbsp;
like Father Callahan from "'Salem's Lot,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.080
Patrick Danville from "Insomnia," and multiple&nbsp;
forms of Randall Flagg from "The Stand."

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:03.040
The way in which Roland meets Patrick Danville&nbsp;
is actually tied to "It." In the final book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:08.160
"The Dark Tower," Roland ventures into the land&nbsp;
of Empathica, where he is taken in by someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:13.360
calling themselves Joe Collins. This person gets&nbsp;
assistance from an android called Stuttering Bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.920
and seems like a nice, funny guy until the&nbsp;
truth sets in. As it turns out, this isn't a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:23.360
former standup comedian called Joe Collins, but&nbsp;
Dandelo, a being that feeds on human emotions.

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:28.480
Dandelo keeps Danville in the basement&nbsp;
where he eats the young man's emotions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:33.360
He gets close to killing Roland, too, but the&nbsp;
gunslinger and his companion manage to defeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.560
the creature. They rescue Danville, and continue&nbsp;
their quest. But the fact that Dandelo feeds on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:43.600
emotion immediately makes the reader think of&nbsp;
Pennywise. According to author Bev Vincent,

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:49.140
"King has stated that Dandelo is not Pennywise,&nbsp;
but they may be from the same species."

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.800
It would seem that having its&nbsp;
heart crushed by Bill Denbrough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:56.800
would be enough to kill Pennywise for&nbsp;
good. After all, once It is murdered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.840
the spell over Derry dissolves, and memories&nbsp;
of Pennywise's evil begin to fade. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:06.900
Stephen King has hinted in subsequent books&nbsp;
that Pennywise may not be gone for good.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.680
"Oh yes, they float."

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.480
In "The Tommyknockers," a character travels to&nbsp;
Derry and has a vision of a familiar-sounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:21.200
clown in a sewer. The character in question isn't&nbsp;
doing well mentally, so he might just be having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.320
a hallucination that just so happens to connect&nbsp;
to one of King's older works. A more substantial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:31.440
hint, though, comes in "Dreamcatcher," a novel&nbsp;
about adults dealing with their past traumas.

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.320
The alien in "Dreamcatcher" is referred to as&nbsp;
Mr. Gray, which is extremely similar to one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:41.520
Pennywise's aliases, of Robert or Bob Gray.&nbsp;
The alien visits Derry in order to poison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:46.000
the water supply but learns that the Derry&nbsp;
Standpipe was destroyed during the events of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.600
"It." On a plaque commemorating those who died&nbsp;
in the 1985 flood, someone has spray-painted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:57.840
words "Pennywise Lives." Was this the result of a&nbsp;
lingering memory in the mind of a Derry resident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:01.920
or a warning that Pennywise isn't done&nbsp;
with the people of Maine? For now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:05.419
that's another question only King&nbsp;
fans can answer for themselves.

